# The ActiveModelSerializers gem's own constants — issue #534 item 6.
#
# The 2026-09-01 corpus opacity sweep counted `ActiveModel::Serializer` 101 times on Mastodon,
# `ActiveModelSerializers::Model` 29 and `ActiveModelSerializers::SerializableResource` 25, every
# one of them resolving to `untyped` because nothing in the bundle named the constant. This file
# names them, and nothing more.
#
# ## The admission rule
#
# The same rule `plugins/rigor-activerecord/sig/active_record/framework.rbs` states: declaring a
# constant makes it RBS-KNOWN, and a known class with an incomplete signature turns every member the
# signature omits into a false `call.undefined-method`. Every class here is therefore ALSO listed in
# the manifest's `open_receivers:` (ADR-26), which is what keeps the members this file does not
# enumerate lenient.
#
# That pairing is load-bearing rather than defensive here, because — unlike the Active Record
# exception hierarchy, which is rescued and not dispatched on — `ActiveModel::Serializer` and
# `ActiveModelSerializers::Model` are exactly the "class a project INHERITS from" shape the Active
# Record file declines to declare. `class REST::AccountSerializer < ActiveModel::Serializer` is what
# an AMS application looks like, and `object`, `scope`, `read_attribute_for_serialization`,
# `attributes`, `has_many` and the rest of the AMS surface are not enumerated below. The Mastodon
# before/after diff recorded in `docs/notes/20260917-ams-object-recognizer.md` is the evidence that
# the pairing holds on a real application.
#
# `ActiveModel` is declared as a module by `rigor-activerecord` as well; RBS reopens a module across
# files, so the two declarations compose rather than collide, and neither plugin depends on the
# other being enabled.

module ActiveModel
  class Serializer
    class CollectionSerializer
    end
  end
end

module ActiveModelSerializers
  class Model
  end

  class SerializableResource
  end

  module Adapter
  end
end
